 "last_activity": "2017-01-20T14:17:16Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "zR1AxMtmCQAJ",
   "from": "B Marques",
   "author": "c56bd39632d05d32",
   "date": "2017-01-20T10:33:04Z",
   "subject": "simmer.optim with schedule resource capacity and multiple generators",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;;font-size:14px\">I&#39;m developing a proof of concept for the simulation of Emergency Department patient flow and resource utilization. The already developed simulation model, using package &quot;simmer&quot;, has multiple generators to reflect hourly variation of patient admissions and also a resource capacity variation based on the time of the day using schedule function.<br></p><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;;font-size:14px\">Now i&#39;m planning to get an optimal solution to the simulated scenario, and as result of my research i&#39;ve get to know the simmer.optim extension. As i was studying it&#39;s usage and funcionalities, i saw that to experiment different solutions to get the optimal one, in the resource definition is necessary to set the capacity to .opt(&quot;xpto&quot;).<br style=\"box-sizing:border-box\">My question is: How can i keep a varying capacity definition during the day (using schedule) at the same time that i want to use the optimization extension? Problem is that the schedule definition needs the resource capacity to be defined has capacity=capacity_schedule for example.</p><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;;font-size:14px\">Thank you in advance for your courtesy. Best Regards,</p><p style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:rgb(51,51,51);font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif,&quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;,&quot;Segoe UI Symbol&quot;;font-size:14px\">Bernardo Marques</p></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "nXsagOVvCQAJ",
   "from": "Iñaki Úcar",
   "author": "0f5b7adb7e70bd8b",
   "date": "2017-01-20T13:19:50Z",
   "subject": "Re: [simmer-devel] simmer.optim with schedule resource capacity and multiple generators",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Hi Bernardo,</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">The <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">.opt(&quot;something&quot;)</code> instruction simply defines a parameter <code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline\">something</code> that the optimiser will substitute with different values. You can place those parameters virtually anywhere in your model. That means that you may try something like this:</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);background-color:rgb(248,248,248);border-radius:3px;display:inline;white-space:pre;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important;display:block;overflow-x:auto;padding:0.5em;color:rgb(51,51,51);background:rgb(248,248,248)\">sim_model &lt;- function() {\r\n  # Schedule x units from 8 to 16 h\r\n  #          y units from 16 to 24 h\r\n  #          z units from 24 to 8 h\r\n  capacity_schedule &lt;- schedule(c(8, 16, 24), c(.opt(&quot;x&quot;), .opt(&quot;y&quot;), .opt(&quot;z&quot;)), period=24)\r\n\r\n  ...\r\n\r\n  env&lt;-simmer() %&gt;%\r\n    add_resource(&quot;my_resource&quot;, capacity_schedule) %&gt;%\r\n    ...\r\n  env\r\n}\r\n</code></pre>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Then you can explore different ranges for x, y, z, i.e., morning, evening and night.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Also note that simmer.optim is in an early stage of development, so please double-check the results you get. And if you find something strange or you have ideas to improve the package, please fill an issue on GitHub.</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">Regards,<br>Iñaki</p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\">2017-01-20 11:33 GMT+01:00 B Marques &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">bernardo...@gmail.com</a>&gt;:</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:0px 0px 1.2em!important\"></p></div></div>"
    ],
